you  our Series a more perfect union aims to  show that what unites us as Americans is  far greater than what divides us nearly  one year after Hurricane Harvey  devastated Houston Texas beyond his home  town many people are still unable to  return to their homes a small group of  military veterans and several dedicated  volunteers are helping to rebuild a  fourth largest city in this country one  home at a time chip Reid went to Houston  to meet them for Courtney column the  opportunity to help displaced Houston  residents return home has helped her  turn her own life around everything felt  that I was doing in life wasn't working  out after four years in the Coast Guard  and suffering from post-traumatic stress  disorders column found Team Rubicon a  Disaster Response organization made up  of military veterans and volunteers  helping to rebuild Houston I figured if  I'm miserable I might as well go help  other people but by helping other people  it pulled me out of that here is our  kitchen the house Courtney is  refurbishing belongs to the Postel  family who had to be evacuated by boat  when the storm hit all of a sudden my  daughter comes running up to us and she  says mom the bedrooms are flooding with  almost two feet of water inside the home  a terrorizing fear set in did you think  it was a lost cause this house at some  point at some point you say never once  have we been flooded and when the water  came in there was no stopping it living  outside the floodplain the pastels had  no flood insurance and lacked the funds  to rebuild on their own help from Team  Rubicon was a dream come true so now  when you come here you see this team of  volunteers from Team Rubicon how does  that make you feel  angel your angel when we thought that  there was no prayer no answer Team  Rubicon came in for Courtney this was  the payoff I had never had so many  people tell me how proud they were of me  and it is amazing from the air Houston  today looks dry and livable but nearly a  year after Harvey struck thousands of  residents are still waiting to return  home Team Rubicon saw that as an  opportunity I didn't see as much of a  response as I had wanted to see former  US Marine Elliot Rios says he felt  compelled to act after the relief  agencies and thousands of volunteers  left Houston in the months after the  storm we are feeling the need for a  group a community of individuals that  would have never been never gotten the  opportunity to go back into their home  Brian kal cono a former Army pilot flew  Blackhawk helicopters during two tours  in Iraq before finding Team Rubicon as a  way to continue to serve Brian what is  the most satisfying part of working for  Team Rubicon for you it doesn't matter  what your background it doesn't matter  what your past is you're here to help  and to be accepted into an organization  like that and have that unconditional  love is is you know it's very hard to  find this day and age I'm so happy to be  home for 67 year old Barbara Herndon who  spent 20 years in this home before being  forced to escape harvey's rising tides  oh my goodness that unconditional love  brought her back home on a recent steamy  afternoon I envisioned I was gonna cry  but I'm not crying thank you God you  don't want to cry no I don't need to cry  after moving Barbara in Team Rubicon  began work on another home the very next  day it's a cycle of veterans helping  others and at the same time helping  themselves thank you for all of the work  you're doing chip Reid CBS News Houston  and that's why we say Houston proud  great story

